      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T01:40:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "VCONN is driven by pp5000_z1.\nthe power sequencer monitors pp3300_z1 but not pp5000_z1 to\ngenerate all_sys_pg. is it safe to assume pp5000_z1 comes\nup with pp3300_z1 upon z1_en_rails?\n\nwould it make sense to wait for S5? i\u0027m not sure it makes\nsense to drive VCONN in g3.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "9aa904242a1f063acdc2b8242764d22da7163f38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67c09cbe_4cdc7772",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T15:05:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003ewould it make sense to wait for S5? i\u0027m not sure it makes\nsense to drive VCONN in g3.\n\nThe power we\u0027ll lose to Vconn in G3 should be offset by the fact that we need to be charging to stay connected to any port partner in G3.\n\nThe primary advantage of sourcing Vconn in G3 is for USB4 platforms because they need to perform mode entry with cables, and this saves them from having to swap back into Vconn source when they boot and go into an alternate mode.  There would also be an advantage if someone ever implements the Charge-Through Vconn Powered devices from the Type-C spec, which would require Vconn to let you charge.  But, as far as I know, no one has actually brought any of those devices to market.",
      "parentUuid": "821808ba_730100ea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "9aa904242a1f063acdc2b8242764d22da7163f38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f0efa2f_6784cd44",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T16:36:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e is it safe to assume pp5000_z1 comes up with pp3300_z1 upon z1_en_rails?\n\nThe power sequencing documentation I found for rex shows that these both come up in response to EN_Z1_Rails. In the diagram they come up at the same time, but it\u0027s looks like these are brought up independently and are just expected to come up at around the same time. There is likely some room for pp3300_z1 to come up a bit before pp5000_z1.\n\nLooking through the schematic, I don\u0027t see any incoming EC signal tied to pp5000_z1. FWIW, looking back at brya, it appears they are basing Vconn swap policy on pp3300_z1 as well. VDD for the power sequencer there is tied to PP3300_Z1, and that drives GPIO_SEQ_EC_DSW_PWROK which brya uses for it\u0027s Vconn swap policy. \n\nI\u0027ll check to see if using `chipset_in_state` to allow Vconn swaps in S5 resolves the PD hard reset issue and followup. If we determine that the pp5000_z1 and pp3300_z1 are not tied closely enough together to use a pp3300_z1-based signal for Vconn swap policy, this may be something to update on brya as well.",
      "parentUuid": "67c09cbe_4cdc7772",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "9aa904242a1f063acdc2b8242764d22da7163f38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a4c0678_a882e308",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-04-28T16:48:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Adding to this,\n\nin the power sequencing documentation I referred to above, `SEQ_EC_ALL_SYS_PG` gets asserted well after PP5000_Z1 and PP3300_Z1 are up. So, by the time the EC allows Vconn swaps with this change, it\u0027s safe to assume PP5000_Z1 is up",
      "parentUuid": "9f0efa2f_6784cd44",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 23,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "9aa904242a1f063acdc2b8242764d22da7163f38",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
